<h1>Briefing Notes – Ethics of AI Institute Meeting</h1>

<p><strong>Date:</strong> September 18, 2026<br>
<strong>Time:</strong> 3:00 PM EST<br>
<strong>Chair:</strong> Matthew Silk<br>
<strong>Attendees:</strong> Members of the Ethics of AI Institute</p>

<div class="section">
<h2>1. White Paper Publication</h2>
  </div>
  
<ul>
    <li>The Institute published its first white paper on AI and agriculture 
    <strong><a href="https://ethicsofaiinstitute.com/research/whitepaper-agriculture.html">Sowing the machine</a></strong>, based on our discussion at the 
    <a href="https://ethicsofaiinstitute.com/meeting/july17meeting.html">last meeting</a>.</li>
    <li>Thank you to Kem-Laurin Lubin for spearheading and compiling the white paper.</li>
    <li>Members expressed interest in continuing the practice of producing white papers from Institute meetings.</li>
</ul>

<div class="section">
<h2>2. Main Discussion: Autonomous Weapons and Drone Warfare</h2>
</div>

<h3>Defining the Technology</h3>

<h4>Semi-autonomous weapons</h4>

<ul>
    <li>Human remains "in the loop.".</li>
    <li>A human authorizes or can override an attack.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Fully autonomous weapons</h4>

<ul>
    <li>Operate without real-time human intervention.</li>
    <li>Can independently identify and engage targets.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Lethal vs. Non-lethal systems</h4>

<ul>
    <li>Lethal systems are designed to inflict physical harm.</li>
    <li>Non-lethal systems may perform surveillance, reconnaissance, or other support functions.</li>
</ul>

The discussion emphasized that ethical concerns differ significantly depending on the level of autonomy and intended use.

<h3>Major Ethical Theme 1: Responsibility and Accountability</h3>

<p>Central question: Who is responsible when an autonomous weapon causes harm?</p>

<h4>Arugment 1: Traditional Military Responsibility Still Applies</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Military command structures already assign responsibility through chains of command.</li>
  <li>Responsibility lies with those who authorize deployment.</li>
  <li>The fact that AI executes the action does not remove human accountability.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Argument 2: AI Complicates Responsibility</h4>

<ul>
  <li>AI may create plausible deniability</li>
  <li>Diffusion of responsibility</li>
  <li>Blame shifting</li>
  <li>Difficulty assigning accountability</li>
</ul>

<p>Potentially responsible parties include:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Political leaders</li>
  <li>Military commanders</li>
  <li>Operators</li>
  <li>Software developers</li>
  <li>AI companies</li>
  <li>Data suppliers</li>
</ul>

<h3>Major Ethical Theme 2: Should Weapons Contain Morality?</h3>

<p>Central question: should autonomous weapons be equipped with explicit moral frameworks.</p>

<h4>Argument 1: Explicit Moral Rules Are Necessary</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Every autonomous weapon already embodies values.</li>
  <li>If moral assumptions are inevitable, they should be made explicit.</li>
  <li>Transparent ethical constraints are preferable to hidden assumptions.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Argument 2: Moral Programming Is Dangerous</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Moral frameworks are culturally dependent.</li>
  <li>Different countries may program conflicting values.</li>
  <li>Governments could embed discriminatory priorities.</li>
  <li>AI could misidentify targets while believing it is acting morally.</li>
  <li>considerable skepticism about whether human moral reasoning can truly be translated into machine logic.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Major Ethical Theme 3: Human vs. Machine Decision Making</h3>

<p>Central question: can AI might make better decisions than humans?</p>

<h4>Potential Benefits of AI Decision Making</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Greater consistency</li>
  <li>Reduced emotional reactions</li>
  <li>Ability to process vast numbers of scenarios</li>
  <li>Possibility of reducing civilian casualties</li>
</ul>

<h4>Concerns with AI Decision Making</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Goal-oriented systems may pursue objectives in unintended ways.</li>
  <li>AI may optimize outcomes without understanding human values.</li>
  <li>A machine's practical effectiveness may conflict with ethical considerations.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Major Ethical Theme 4: Autonomous Weapons as a Defensive Tool</h3>

<p>Central question: can AI improve defensive capabilities?</p>

<h4>Potential Advantages</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Can help smaller states defend against larger powers.</li>
  <li>inexpensive drones have enabled countries with fewer resources to resist militarily stronger opponents.</li>
  <li>May reduce military asymmetries.</li>
  <li>Have already changed modern warfare.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Concerns</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Widespread access could fuel arms races.</li>
  <li>Non-state actors may gain access.</li>
  <li>Lower costs may make war easier to initiate.</li>
  <li>The technology could "gamify" warfare by distancing decision-makers from human consequences.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Major Ethical Theme 5: The "Arms Race" Question</h3>

<p>Central question: do governments have a moral obligation to develop AI weapons if adversaries are doing so?</p>

<h4>Arguments for Development</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Refusing to develop the technology could leave nations vulnerable.</li>
  <li>Development helps create defensive countermeasures.</li>
  <li>Technological preparedness may act as deterrence.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Arguments Against</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Development itself contributes to proliferation.</li>
  <li>New weapons often normalize future escalation.</li>
  <li>History shows technological races can increase conflict rather than security (e.g., nuclear weapons).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Major Ethical Theme 6: Regulation and Governance</h3>

<p>The group broadly agreed that self-regulation by private companies is insufficient.</p>

<h4>Key Concerns</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Social media as an example of failed self-regulation.</li>
  <li>Financial incentives may conflict with public safety.</li>
  <li>Military AI development is increasingly tied to large technology firms.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Proposed Solutions</h4>

<ul>
  <li>Dedicated government agencies focused on AI oversight.</li>
  <li>Interdisciplinary regulatory bodies.</li>
  <li>Greater transparency and disclosure requirements.</li>
  <li>Independent oversight mechanisms.</li>
  <li>Stronger democratic accountability.</li>
</ul>

<p>However, participants also acknowledged concerns about governments themselves being influenced by corporate interests or political agendas.</p>

<h4>Institutional Trust</h4>

<p>A recurring theme was a growing lack of public trust.</p>

<ul>
  <li>Can private companies be trusted?</li>
  <li>Can governments be trusted?</li>
  <li>Who should govern powerful AI systems?</li>
  <li>Society is experiencing a broader crisis of trust, making AI governance especially difficult.</li>
  <li>Governance systems themselves may require reform before citizens will have confidence in AI regulation.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Technology, Power, and Geopolitics</h4>

<p>The discussion explored how AI intersects with global power structures.</p>

<p>Topics included:</p>

<ul>
  <li>U.S.-China technological competition</li>
  <li>Military-industrial relationships</li>
  <li>The role of technology companies in defense</li>
  <li>Narratives about an "AI race" as rhetoric or real geopolitical competition</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. Future Activities</h2>

<h4>Publications</h4>

<p>The group discussed creating a future publication based on the autonomous warfare discussion.</p>

<h4>Next Meeting</h4> October 2, 2026

<h4>Potential Future Topics</h4>

<ul>
  <li>AGI and existential risk ("AI is going to kill us all")</li>
  <li>AI-generated intimate images and related harms</li>
  <li>Child safety and AI-generated content</li>
  <li>AI in education</li>
  <li>Cognitive impacts of AI use</li>
  <li>Surveillance technologies (including Flock systems)</li>
  <li>Civic AI literacy and transparency</li>
</ul>

